CATEGORIES:Youth & Family Activities
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/america-250-at-carolina"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041327Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53993106248651
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a series of events to commemorate America's 250th a
 nniversary at the nation's first public university. The events run from Oc
 t. 14 to 23 and include performances\, poetry readings\, lectures\, panel 
 discussions and more. Most of the events are free and open to the public. 
 \n\n \n\nVisit America 250 at Carolina on the history website. \n\n \n\nSc
 hedule of Events\n\n \n\nWednesday\, October 14\n\n4:30-6 p.m. (Flyleaf Bo
 oks) – Discussion with Antwain Hunter (History) on Frederick Douglass’
 s What to the Slave is the Fourth of July (1852) with Aisha Booze-Hall (Ja
 ckson Center)\nPlease purchase tickets for this event.\n\n \n\nMonday\, Oc
 tober 19\n\n \n\n12:30-1 p.m. (Pleasants Room\, Wilson Library) – Openin
 g Keynote by Pamela Brewington Cashwell (Secretary\, NCDC)\, North Carolin
 a at 250\n\n \n\n1-2 p.m. (Pleasants Room) – Preserving North Carolina
 ’s History for the General Public\, a panel discussion with Sec. Pamela 
 Brewington Cashwell\, Darin Waters (Dep. Sec. Office of Archives and Histo
 ry)\, and Biff Hollingsworth (Wilson Library)\n\n \n\n2:15-3:30 p.m. (Plea
 sants Room\, Wilson Library) – Teaching the Foundations of American Demo
 cracy in K-12 Schools\, a panel discussion with Cori Greer Banks (Carolina
  K-12\, CPH)\, Jillian La Serna (EPIC/Public Policy)\; and Mike Henning (C
 arrboro Elementary)\n\n \n\n3:40-3:45 p.m. (Pleasants Room\, Wilson Librar
 y) – Poetry Reading by Alexis Clifton (English and Creative Writing clas
 s of 2027)\, You Say the New American Dream is to Leave/Honeysuckle Dosage
 .\n\n \n\n3:45-5 p.m. (Pleasants Room\, Wilson Library) – Poetry and Lib
 erty\, a panel discussion with Tyree Daye (English and Comp Lit) Eliza Ric
 hards (English and Comp Lit)\, and Denise Xu (English and Comp Lit)\n\n \n
 \n6-7:30 p.m. (Stone Center Auditorium) – Welcome by LeRhonda S. Manigau
 lt-Bryant (Director\, Sonja Haynes Stone Center for Research in Black Cult
 ure and History). Poetry Reading and Conversation with Natasha Trethewey (
 Pulitzer-Prize Winning Former U.S. Poet Laureate)\, moderated by Michael A
 . Betts II (High Point University) and Miguel La Serna (Chair\, History). 
 SOLD OUT\n\n \n\nTuesday\, October 20\n\n10 a.m. - Noon (Pleasants Room\, 
 Wilson Library) – Great American Stories: Food\, Music\, and Folklore of
  the American South with Amanda J. Cobb (American Studies)\, Kelly Alexand
 er (American Studies)\, and Gabrielle Berlinger (American Studies)\n\n \n\
 n2:30-4 p.m. (Pleasants Room\, Wilson Library) – Lecture and conversatio
 n with David Crabtree (CEO and General Manager\, PBS North Carolina)\, mod
 erated by Michael A. Betts II\n\n \n\n5-5:30 p.m. (Playmakers Lobby) – C
 olonial Fiddling by Eli Howells\n\n \n\n5:30-7 p.m. (Playmakers) – Lectu
 re by Mandy Cooper (History) Hamilton in Historical Context. Conversation 
 with Daveed Diggs\, a Tony\, Grammy\, and Lucille Lortel Award-winning a
 ctor\, writer\, producer\, and musician best known for originating the dua
 l roles of Thomas Jefferson and Marquis de Lafayette in the original cast 
 of the Broadway hit Hamilton\, earning a Tony Award for his performance. M
 oderated by Michael A. Betts II (High Point University)\, Miguel La Serna 
 (Chair\, History)\, and Katherine Hunter Williams (Chair\, Dramatic Arts).
  Limited tickets will be available by sponsoring departments.\n\n \n\nWedn
 esday\, October 21\n\n2:30-3:30 p.m. (Pleasants Room\, Wilson Library) –
  Lecture by Katherine Turk (History)\, “All Men Would be Tyrants if They
  Could”: Women and Power in Revolutionary America\n\n \n\n3:45-4:30 p.m.
  (Pleasants Room\, Wilson Library) – Performance by Singer-Songwriter Da
 wn Landes\, The Liberated Woman’s Songbook\n\n \n\n6-7 p.m. (Person Reci
 tal Hall) – Welcome by Jocelyn Neal (Chair\, Music). Lecture and convers
 ation with John Wood Sweet (History)\, Bancroft-Prize winning author of Th
 e Sewing Girl’s Tale\, moderated by Michael A. Betts II and Miguel La Se
 rna (Chair\, History)\n\n \n\n7-8:30 p.m. (Person Recital Hall) – Mendin
 g Sampler: Lanah Sawyer and the Cost of Courage in Revolutionary America\,
  a concert featuring C. Leonard Raybon (Director of Choirs\, Composer at T
 ulane University\; Founder of Sacred Nine Project)\, Jeanne Fisher (Music)
 \, E. Katherine Hargrove (Colonial Williamsburg interpreter)\, and undergr
 aduate performers (Music)\n\n \n\nThursday\, October 22\n\n2:30-3:30 p.m. 
 (University Room\, Hyde Hall) – Lecture by Wayne E. Lee (History)\, The 
 Cutting-Off Way: Indigenous Warfare in the American Revolution\n\n \n\n3:4
 5-4:45 p.m. (University Room\, Hyde Hall) – Lecture by Amanda J. Cobb (A
 merican Studies)\, Indigenous Perspectives on America 250 Memory and Comme
 moration\n\n \n\n6:30 – 8:30 p.m. (Fearrington Reading Room\, Wilson Lib
 rary) Lecture by Pulitzer Prize winner Kathleen DuVal (History)\, author o
 f Native Nations\, A Millenium in North America. \n\n \n\nFriday\, October
  23\n\n11:30 a.m. – 12:30 p.m. (Love House) – Southern Cultures: Black
  Freedom Narratives in Place\, Publication\, and Performance\, a conversat
 ion between Michelle Lanier (Director\, North Carolina Historic Sites)\, D
 anielle Purifoy (Geography)and artist and activist Toshi Reagon (NYFA)\n\n
 1–2 p.m. (Love House) – Lecture by Danita Mason-Hogans (Southern Oral 
 History Program)\, The Role of Oral History and Community Memory in Interp
 reting Early American History and its Long Afterlives\n\n \n\n2:15-3:30 p.
 m. (Love House) – SOHP and Civil Rights: Partnering with LDF\nNAACP Lega
 l Defense Fund (LDF) archivists and SOHP oral historians and project leade
 rs will explore freedom as an ongoing and active pursuit\n\n \n\n4-5:30 p.
 m. (Moeser Performance Hall) Theatrical Performance and Conversation with 
 Mike Wiley (Director and Artist in Residence for the Kenan Institute for E
 thics)\,One Noble Journey: A Box Marked Freedom\n\n \n\n7:30-8:30 p.m. (Mo
 eser Performance Hall) Concert by LaToya Lain (Music)\, Narrative of a Sla
 ve Woman\, moderated by Michael A. Betts II\n\n \n\n \n\nThe events of Ame
 rica 250 at Carolina are presented to you by:\n\nCollege of Arts and Scien
 ces | Center for the Study of the American South | Department of Dramatic 
 Art | Department of English | Department of History | Institute for the Ar
 ts and Humanities | Department of Music | Department of Political Science 
 | Office of the Provost | Department of Public Policy | Sonja Haynes Stone
  Center for Research in Black Culture and History | University Library\n\n
  \n\nAdditional support for America 250 at Carolina is provided by:\n\nDep
 artment for African\, African American\, and Diaspora Studies | Carolina P
 erforming Arts | Carolina Public Humanities | Curriculum in Peace\, War\, 
 and Defense | Department of Sociology | Southern Cultures | Southern Oral 
 History Program | Triangle Early American History Seminar
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:America 250 at Carolina
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/america-250-at-carolina
CATEGORIES:Performances
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/cyber-bowl-3024"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041327Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53991505494200
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
DESCRIPTION:It's time to play the third annual Cyber Bowl\, a month-long co
 mpetition to determine which North Carolina university is the most cyber s
 avvy. \n\n \n\nAll October long\, students\, faculty and staff can log int
 o the Cyber Bowl website and take a short cybersecurity quiz. Each complet
 ed quiz earns a point for Carolina.\n\n \n\nUNC-Chapel Hill faces off agai
 nst nine other North Carolina universities with a lot to prove and two win
 s to defend. In the inaugural Cyber Bowl\, Carolina won the championship b
 y garnering the largest participation by percentage. Last year\, Carolina 
 fielded the most players — almost 2\,000 — of any school.\n\n \n\nThe 
 Cyber Bowl is sponsored by Information Technology Services and the Informa
 tion Security Office as part of National Cybersecurity Awareness Month.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Play the Cyber Bowl
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/cyber-bowl-3024
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/unc-research-week"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041327Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53931039941435
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
DESCRIPTION:UNC Research Week (URW) will return October 19-23\, 2026\, with
  the theme “What’s Next?”\n \n\nThis year\, we will showcase emergin
 g ideas\, discoveries\, and innovations at Carolina that are shaping our f
 uture. Join us in highlighting the Carolina research community’s commitm
 ent to advancing knowledge that has the potential to transform industries\
 , strengthen communities\, and create a better world for us all. From brea
 kthroughs in materials science to novel AI applications to creative and cu
 ltural exploration\, we’re diving into endeavors that are at the frontie
 r of what’s known.\n \n\nUNC Research Week is an annual\, campus-wide in
 itiative sponsored by the Office of the Vice Chancellor for Research and t
 he Office for Undergraduate Research. Through a week-long series of events
  hosted by units across campus\, the community will come together for work
 shops\, forums\, and fairs that celebrate UNC-CH’s work and inspire the 
 next generation.\n\nClick here for a list of URW events
LOCATION:Campus Wide
SUMMARY:UNC Research Week
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/unc-research-week
END:VEVENT
