BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ai-and-the-future-of-social-work-exploring-opportunities-and-ethical-questions"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041327Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54159913887710
DTSTART:20261020T163000Z
DTEND:20261020T173000Z
DESCRIPTION:When thinking about the future of AI\, social work may not be t
 he first field that comes to mind. However\, researchers and educators at 
 UNC’s School of Social Work have long been engaged in AI-related project
 s and have unique insights to share about how the technology can be used t
 o address important social problems.\n\n \n\nThis panel discussion between
  School of Social Work faculty will explore current and potential uses of 
 AI in social work research and education and key ethical considerations re
 lated to its use in the field. The panelists include experts with more tha
 n a decade of experience in AI-involved research as well as relatively rec
 ent adopters of the technology.\n\n \n\nThis is a hybrid event. Join us at
  https://unc.zoom.us/j/96298278191.\n\n \n\nThe panel will be moderated by
  Kathy Colville\, Associate Dean for Community Engagement and Outreach\, a
 nd feature the following panelists:\n\n \n\nEric Rice\nDistinguished Profe
 ssor\nAssociate Dean of Research and Faculty Development\nGordon Berg and 
 Martha Beach Chair in Community Social Work\n\nRice’s current work focus
 es on the application of AI technologies to assist persons living with hom
 elessness and the organizations that serve them. For the past decade\, he 
 has worked with colleagues in computer science to merge social work scienc
 e and AI\, seeking novel solutions to major social problems such as homele
 ssness\, suicide\, and HIV. In 2016\, along with Milind Tambe\, he founded
  the University of Southern California’s Center for AI in Society\, the 
 world’s first university-based center merging AI and social work.\n\n \n
 \nHeidi McNeilly\nClinical Assistant Professor\, Adams School of Dentistry
 \nAdjunct Assistant Professor\, School of Social Work\n\nMcNeilly’s work
  involves integrating social work services into dental education to enhanc
 e patient care and improve access for underserved populations\, and her cu
 rrent research focus is the integration of AI into education. To this end\
 , she recently led a team that developed an AI chatbot to help dental stud
 ents build patient communication skills. In 2026\, McNeilly was named the 
 UNC Center for Faculty Excellence’s Faculty Fellow for Generative AI and
  Clinical Education. The fellowship will support her efforts to build reso
 urces related to the instructional needs and challenges presented by gener
 ative AI for faculty members campuswide.\n\n \n\nHsun-Ta Hsu\nAssociate Pr
 ofessor & John A. Tate Early Career Scholar\, School of Social Work\nAssoc
 iate Professor\, School of Data and Information Sciences\n\nHsu has long b
 een interested in how artificial intelligence can help people make complex
  decisions with consistency\, but he also recognizes the ethical and fairn
 ess challenges that AI-aided decision-making may pose for disenfranchised 
 communities. His recent work adopts a community-engaged data science appro
 ach to promoting health and housing outcomes among individuals experiencin
 g homelessness by engaging stakeholders to co-develop AI tools that addres
 s their most pressing needs in ways that are both efficient and equitable.
  Hsu also teaches courses on AI\, including a new offering entitled AI for
  Social Good and Social Work Practice: Ethical and Applied Perspectives.\n
 \n \n\nStefanie Panke\nDirector of Digital Learning and Innovation\, Schoo
 l of Social Work\n\nPanke is actively engaged in researching\, promoting\,
  and advocating for the responsible and effective integration of AI—part
 icularly generative AI—into educational practices. Her work includes res
 earch on GenAI in academic writing\, teaching\, and instructional design\,
  with contributions to a Journal of Teacher Education special issue and an
  edited\, student-authored e-book on GenAI in education. Recently\, Panke 
 and School of Social Work colleague Tina Souders were awarded a 2025–26 
 CFE/Lenovo Instructional Innovation Grant from the UNC Center for Faculty 
 Excellence to support the School’s online MSW program through the use of
  AI to assist in the development of high-quality\, immersive\, and accessi
 ble multimedia learning materials\, particularly case studies and simulati
 ons.
GEO:35.907102;-79.054247
LOCATION:Tate-Turner-Kuralt Building\, Auditorium
SUMMARY:AI and the Future of Social Work: Exploring Opportunities and Ethic
 al Questions
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ai-and-the-future-of-social-wo
 rk-exploring-opportunities-and-ethical-questions
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
