X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/het-talks-7596"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041327Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54197567219057
DTSTART:20261022T160000Z
DTEND:20261022T170000Z
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy presentations by this year’s recipients of the Phillip 
 and Ruth Hettleman Prizes for Scholarly Achievement. The talks will provid
 e an engaging look into these distinguished early career scholars’ work.
  The late Phillip Hettleman\, a member of the Carolina class of 1921\, and
  his wife Ruth established their prestigious named award in 1986 to recogn
 ize the achievements of outstanding junior faculty.\n\n \n\nThere are five
  winners of the 2026 Hettleman Prizes. This Het Talks event features two o
 f them:\nAndrea Knittel\, Obstetrics and Gynecology\, School of Medicine\n
 Knittel’s research is reshaping both scientific understanding and clinic
 al practice by bringing attention to a historically understudied populatio
 n and developing evidence-based approaches to improve care. Her findings h
 ave influenced how data on reproductive health in carceral settings are in
 terpreted and have informed innovative interventions that prioritize treat
 ment over incarceration. By integrating research\, clinical care\, and pol
 icy innovation\, her work has the potential to improve health outcomes for
  individuals and their families while driving system-level changes in heal
 th and justice systems.\n\n \n\nCarmina Valle\, Nutrition\, Gillings Schoo
 l of Global Public Health\nValle’s work is advancing cancer prevention a
 nd survivorship care by demonstrating how digital tools can deliver scalab
 le and cost-efficient health interventions. Her research has shaped effect
 ive practices for using social media\, mobile health technologies\, and pe
 rsonalized messaging to improve health behaviors\, reduce cancer risk and 
 enhance quality of life among cancer survivors. By translating behavioral 
 science into practical\, widely deployable solutions\, her work is helping
  address major public health challenges and expanding access to impactful 
 interventions across populations.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Het Talks
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/het-talks-7596
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/het-talks-lecture-series"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041327Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54241467009158
DTSTART:20261022T160000Z
DTEND:20261022T170000Z
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy presentations by this year’s recipients of the Phillip 
 and Ruth Hettleman Prizes for Scholarly Achievement. The Het Talks will pr
 ovide an engaging look into these distinguished early career scholars’ w
 ork. The late Phillip Hettleman\, a member of the Carolina class of 1921\,
  and his wife Ruth established their prestigious named award in 1986 to re
 cognize the achievements of outstanding junior faculty.\n\n \n\nThe Het Ta
 lks virtual lectures are broken up across two days. Registration is requir
 ed and these are CLE Credit events.\n\n \n\nOctober 19\, 2026 from 12 - 1 
 pm\nDaniel Dominguez\, Pharmacology\, School of Medicine\nDominguez’s wo
 rk is reshaping the field of RNA biology by providing foundational tools a
 nd concepts for decoding RNA regulation and its role in health and disease
 . His discoveries are widely used by other researchers to interpret diseas
 e-causing mutations and understand molecular mechanisms underlying conditi
 ons like cancer. By uncovering how RNA regulation can go wrong\, his resea
 rch is opening new avenues for therapeutic development and advancing a dee
 per understanding of fundamental biological processes.\n\n \n\nAndrew Mann
 \, Physics and Astronomy\, College of Arts and Sciences\nMann’s research
  is helping define one of the most exciting frontiers in astrophysics: und
 erstanding how planetary systems\, including those that could support life
 \, form and evolve. His discoveries have significantly expanded the known 
 population of young exoplanets and provided critical data for next-generat
 ion space missions searching for habitable worlds. By developing tools tha
 t have been widely adopted by others in the field\, and by making high-imp
 act discoveries used by astronomers worldwide\, Mann and his team are shap
 ing the direction of exoplanet science for years to come.\n\n \n\nOctober 
 22\, 2026 from 12 - 1 pm\n\nAndrea Knittel\, Obstetrics and Gynecology\, S
 chool of Medicine\nKnittel’s research is reshaping both scientific under
 standing and clinical practice by bringing attention to a historically und
 erstudied population and developing evidence-based approaches to improve c
 are. Her findings have influenced how data on reproductive health in carce
 ral settings are interpreted and have informed innovative interventions th
 at prioritize treatment over incarceration. By integrating research\, clin
 ical care\, and policy innovation\, her work has the potential to improve 
 health outcomes for individuals and their families while driving system-le
 vel changes in health and justice systems.\n\n \n\nCarmina Valle\, Nutriti
 on\, Gillings School of Global Public Health\nValle’s work is advancing 
 cancer prevention and survivorship care by demonstrating how digital tools
  can deliver scalable and cost-efficient health interventions. Her researc
 h has shaped effective practices for using social media\, mobile health te
 chnologies\, and personalized messaging to improve health behaviors\, redu
 ce cancer risk and enhance quality of life among cancer survivors. By tran
 slating behavioral science into practical\, widely deployable solutions\, 
 her work is helping address major public health challenges and expanding a
 ccess to impactful interventions across populations.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Het Talks Lecture Series
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/het-talks-lecture-series
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
