BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-bettie-allison-rand-lectures-in-art-history-alexander-marr-university-of-cambridge"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041329Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54159871526770
DTSTART:20261103T223000Z
DTEND:20261104T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:Pictorial Intelligence in Renaissance Art: Holbein’s Wit\n\n 
 \n\nThe Renaissance witnessed intense debate about the nature and location
  of human intelligence. When it came to artists\, this was focused on natu
 ral endowments versus trained skill\, the relationship between the mind an
 d the hand\, and—especially among humanists—whether pictorial art was 
 capable of intelligent authorship as opposed to mere replication of the vi
 sible world. Taking Hans Holbein the Younger as a case study\, this lectur
 e will explore how the mindful hand of a supremely gifted painter could de
 monstrate the inherent ingenuity and playful wit of an inventive--rather t
 han merely mimetic--artistic intelligence. \n\n \n\nAlexander Marr is Univ
 ersity Professor of Renaissance and Early Modern Art. He specializes in Eu
 ropean art 1400-1800\, especially its intellectual and literary aspects in
  their social contexts. His most recent publication is Holbein's Wit: Pict
 orial Ingenuity in Renaissance Art.\n\n \n\nBefore coming to Cambridge\, h
 e taught at the University of Southern California and the University of St
  Andrews. From 2014 to 2019\, he was Director of the project Genius before
  Romanticism: Ingenuity in Early Modern Art & Science\, funded by an ERC C
 onsolidator Grant. His awards include a Paul Mellon Centre Senior Fellowsh
 ip\, the Robert H. Smith Residency at the V&A\, and a Philip Leverhulme Pr
 ize. He was the founding Director of Cambridge Visual Culture (CVC). In 20
 24\, he delivered the Pharos Lectures at the University of Oxford.\n\n \n\
 nThrough a generous gift to the UNC Arts and Sciences Foundation\, William
  G. Rand established this lecture series in memory of his late wife\, Bett
 ie Allison Rand. This funding enables the Department of Art and Art Histor
 y to invite one or more eminent art historians to UNC-CH every other year 
 for residencies of various lengths. While they are in Chapel Hill\, these 
 scholars present a series of lectures and interact with undergraduate and 
 graduate art history and studio art students.\n\n \n\nA weeknight or dayti
 me permit is now required after 5:00 pm on weekdays. No permit is required
  from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:30 am Monday. A $1.00 one-night pass is ava
 ilable in selected lots. More information can be found HERE.\n\n \n\nConta
 ct: Christoph Brachmann\, cbrachma@email.unc.edu   \n\n \n\nThis event is 
 eligible for CLE credit.
GEO:35.913407;-79.052875
LOCATION:Hyde Hall\, University Room
SUMMARY:The Bettie Allison Rand Lectures in Art History: Alexander Marr\, U
 niversity of Cambridge
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-bettie-allison-rand-lectur
 es-in-art-history-alexander-marr-university-of-cambridge
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
