BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Meetup Data Science Nc": "https://www.meetup.com/data-science-nc/events/316868180/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:meetup_data_science_nc
X-SOURCE:Meetup: Data Science Raleigh
UID:event_316868180@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261017T041241Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261111T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261111T150000
SUMMARY:Business Context in AI Initiatives: Why It Matters More Than You T
 hink
DESCRIPTION:Data Science Raleigh\nAI initiatives depend on data—but data
  without business context can lead to inconsistent answers\, unreliable in
 sights\, and decisions that miss the mark.\n\nWhat does “customer\,” 
 “revenue\,” or even “active” actually mean across your organizatio
 n? The answer can vary by team\, system\, or business function.\n\nWhile t
 hese inconsistencies have long created challenges for data and analytics t
 eams\, the rise of AI makes the stakes even higher.\n\nIn this expert pane
 l\, moderated by Jennifer Jackson\, Chief Marketing Officer at Actian\, we
 ’ll explore why business context has become a critical foundation for tr
 usted AI. Hear perspectives on how organizations can align definitions\, m
 etrics\, ownership\, and relationships across their data environment—and
  make that context accessible to the AI experiences employees and leaders 
 increasingly rely on.\n\nThe session will also feature a live walkthrough 
 demonstrating how modern data platforms can activate business metrics and 
 context with less manual effort\, along with practical questions to consid
 er when evaluating whether your data and AI foundation is ready to scale.\
 n\n**What You'll Learn:**\n\n1️⃣ Why business context matters beyond t
 raditional data and technical teams\n\n2️⃣ How incomplete\, inconsiste
 nt\, or siloed business definitions can impact AI initiatives\n\n3️⃣ H
 ow trusted and up-to-date business context can improve the relevance and r
 eliability of AI-driven insights\n\n4️⃣ What it looks like to operatio
 nalize business context without creating additional manual overhead\n\n5
 ️⃣ Key questions to ask when evaluating your organization's data and A
 I readiness\n\n**Who Should Attend**\n\nBusiness\, data\, analytics\, and 
 technology leaders responsible for developing\, scaling\, or operationaliz
 ing AI initiatives.\n\n[Register here](https://www.airmeet.com/e/fd37f6f0-
 c16f-11f1-b5d0-4566c48b1cdf)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/data-science-nc/events/316868180/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20261006T131015Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T131015Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/methods-for-conducting-qualitative-coding-and-data-analysis"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041329Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52639793042757
DTSTART:20261111T150000Z
DTEND:20261111T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:Knowing how to analyze data from qualitative research studies t
 o respond to complex health and social priorities is rewarding\, but it ca
 n also be daunting. This short course provides students practical skills f
 or coding and analyzing qualitative data for research studies in U.S. and 
 international settings. The short course uses a lab- and discussion-based 
 approach. There will be four modules. In the 1st module\, students will le
 arn about the practical steps for qualitative coding and data analysis\, i
 ncluding learning about concepts and techniques relating to how to iterati
 vely read narrative data\, how to develop codes\, how to create a codebook
 \, how to consider hierarchal coding structures\, and how to formulate the
 mes from codes. As part of this module\, students will also learn about th
 ree types of analytical approaches: content analysis\, thematic analysis\,
  and discourse analysis. In the 2nd module\, students will learn about how
  to use an online qualitative analysis software tool\, including how to im
 port a qualitative dataset and codebook and how to assign codes to text. W
 e will review a real-world example of a coded qualitative dataset to discu
 ss techniques for obtaining code extracts\, memos\, matrices\, and other a
 nalytical tools. In the 3rd module\, students will read three qualitative 
 transcripts from a topic selected by the instructor and work in small grou
 ps to develop an abbreviated codebook and assign codes to the test transcr
 ipts. Students will work in real-time using an online qualitative analysis
  software. In the final module\, students will learn about various ways to
  present findings from qualitative data analyses. We will critique and dis
 cuss various styles for presenting qualitative research\, such as using il
 lustrative quotations\, visual displays\, or quantified qualitative result
 s. Students will learn about the inherent advantages and disadvantages of 
 these styles when presenting their own qualitative findings. This course i
 s designed to provide new knowledge for both introductory and intermediate
  students.\n\nRegistration and fee are required for Zoom link access.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Methods for Conducting Qualitative Coding and Data Analysis
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/methods-for-conducting-qualita
 tive-coding-and-data-analysis
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
