BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-taylor-barnes-weight-of-light"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261017T041329Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54187615218771
DTSTART:20261118T130000Z
DTEND:20261118T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Reception and artist conversation: October 29\, 2026\, 5:00 pm\
 n\n \n\nWeight of Light is an exhibition of seven works by multidisciplina
 ry artist taylor barnes\, created during her year-long residency at West O
 rchid in Pine Lake\, Georgia. Weight of Light considers the relationship b
 etween material\, perception\, and the ongoing process of becoming. Emergi
 ng from a period of experimentation and personal reflection\, the works oc
 cupy an ambiguous space between painting and object. Neither fixed represe
 ntations nor resolved compositions\, the works function as thresholds—si
 tes of transition where questions of origin\, transformation\, and self-po
 ssession remain open.\n\n \n\nbarnes approaches the canvas as a material s
 tructure rather than a passive surface. Through staining\, layering\, satu
 ration\, and the transformation of matter between solid and liquid states\
 , she explores the physical and conceptual possibilities of charcoal. Mate
 rials shift\, settle\, resist\, and respond\, creating conditions in which
  chance\, absence\, and emergence become integral to the work. The works d
 o not seek resolution through conventional notions of completion. Instead\
 , they retain evidence of their making—gestures\, interruptions\, accumu
 lations\, and erasures that reveal the instability and possibility inheren
 t in transformation. What remains visible is a record of decisions\, encou
 nters\, and material negotiations. The works resist linear narratives of p
 rogress\, suggesting instead a cyclical understanding of origin\, movement
 \, and revelation. Abstract maps\, they trace an unfolding relationship be
 tween the known and obscured. Darkness and light emerge not as opposing fo
 rces\, but as interdependent conditions which continually refigure percept
 ion and understanding. Black\, the central color and philosophical foundat
 ion of barnes' practice\, becomes a space of depth\, presence\, and possib
 ility—a means of considering how material holds identity\, memory\, and 
 lived experience. Informed by bell hooks' writings on objectification and 
 subjectivity\, barnes' work engages questions of agency\, visibility\, and
  the reclamation of self. Ultimately\, Weight of Light proposes ascension 
 not as transcendence or arrival\, but as an ongoing practice of self-posse
 ssion. Through material experimentation and an embrace of uncertainty\, ba
 rnes creates space for the possibility that becoming is neither linear nor
  complete\, but continually negotiated through acts of making\, unmaking\,
  and reclamation.\n\n \n\ntaylor barnes is a multidisciplinary artist work
 ing across ceramics\, fibers\, and drawing. Her practice investigates spir
 ituality\, oral history\, and structures of power through material and for
 m. Working primarily with clay\, fabric\, string\, and charcoal\, she tran
 slates emotion and lived experience into material forms that hold traces o
 f labor\, memory\, and ritual. barnes received her MFA in Fibers from the 
 University of North Texas. She lives and works between Georgia and Austin\
 , Texas.\n\n \n\nAdmission: Free\n\nGallery Hours: Monday-Friday\, 8 am-5 
 pm\n\n \n\nA weeknight or daytime permit is now required after 5:00 pm on 
 weekdays. No permit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:30 am Monday
 . A $1.00 one-night pass is available in selected lots. More information c
 an be found HERE.\n\n \n\nArtist website: https://www.barnesartistry.com/ 
 \n\n \n\nFor more information\, please contact: Annette Lawrence\, annette
 l@unc.edu \n\n \n\nImage credit: Courtesy of the artist
GEO:35.912392;-79.054366
LOCATION:Hanes Art Center\, John and June Allcott Gallery
SUMMARY:John and June Allcott Gallery: taylor barnes\, Weight of Light
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-
 taylor-barnes-weight-of-light
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
